great well thank you my name is Katie  dykes I'm the  Commissioner for energy at the  Connecticut Department of Energy and  Environmental Protection and I'm just  really grateful to the Yale climate  energy institute and to Senator Murphy  for convening us today and and  especially impressed by all of you for  returning out I am a recovering a long  time Yale student and it wasn't that  long ago but i can't remember friday  classes ever being very popular let  alone voluntarily showing up on a friday  afternoon this late so i think it is a  testament to the importance of this  topic and appreciate the great turnout  and support for talking about this issue  you know i'm really struck it's here at  hearing some of the other some of the  presentations and obviously the emphasis  on the need to have a much more  resilient infrastructure if we're going  to adapt to climate change and i think  that my experience i came to work at  deep which is our acronym about a year  and a half ago and and one of the things  that has really struck me is that if  we're going to have our regulatory and  policy planning system encourage and  support a more resilient infrastructure  it has to start with the design of the  the government entity itself and  connecticut has taken a really important  and innovative step in trying to make  sure that climate change is integrated  into all aspects of our policy and  planning through the reorganization that  occurred in july 2011 that created the  department where i work essentially you  took to take the federal analogue that  it would have been the EPA and you  merged it with FERC and added a DOA on  top and mushed it all together and and  that's essentially what was created with  our new department we took the former  Department of Environmental Protection  here in Connecticut merged it with the  public utilities control authority and  then created a new policy energy policy  Bureau and through this this or  reorganization you know what's happened  is that  now instead of having separate silos  where we think about environmental  policy and regulation over here and then  we regulate utilities over here and  think about energy issues now we're  challenged to do that all in one in one  structure and it's very exciting it's  very it's very challenging but I think  we're starting to see some of the  results of that that new organization  being reflected in the policies that  we're developing here in the state  governor malloy has been a real has  championed a vision of cheaper and  cleaner and more reliable energy for  Connecticut through a variety of  different initiatives that we've been  working on my part of the department  focuses on the energy aspects and  climate change has been integral to to  all of that we have a very critical task  here in adapting our our energy system  to to climate change we're doubling down  on our investment in energy efficiency  here in Connecticut and we are engaged  right now in procuring additional  renewables to increase the the  cleanliness of our energy supply we have  really been pursuing this on a whole  range of fronts Connecticut has been a  leader in something that's called the  Regional Greenhouse Gas Initiative and  we're very pleased to see now the EPA  and and leadership in Washington  including Senator Murphy ensuring that  other states are going to start taking  steps to clean up the energy our  electricity system so that other states  can catch up to what we've been doing  here in New England and Connecticut but  as I said governor moyes you know vision  it it's cheaper cleaner and more  reliable and each one of those  components is is absolutely important  it's no easy a task to accomplish this  in Connecticut where we have some of the  highest electricity prices in the  continental US and it's no easy task in  a state where we have had to experience  the the major storms that have been  detailed by some of  the panel is so far in 2011 2012  hurricane sandy tropical storm irene the  october snow storm that knocked out  power to 800,000 of the 1.2 million  customers of just one of our utility  companies here in the state you know I  know senator Murphy had observed about  you know we may not see the the fabric  of democracy unraveling here in  Connecticut as a result of drought or  some of these other impacts but if you  cut off power and electricity to homes  and businesses across the state and  people are without a shower and a dead  cell phone and and and sitting in the  cold it may not be long before you start  having armed revolution so and not to be  light about it but it the economic  impacts of storm outages are no joke the  the safety concerns the impacts on  public health are very severe and so for  that reason taking our the governor's  vision of cheaper cleaner more reliable  we've sat down to say you know what can  we do to ensure that our energy  infrastructure is prepared for climate  change and so we've been pursuing an  aggressive multi-faceted strategy to  improve the resiliency of our electric  grid through hardening transmission and  distribution lines undergrounding those  lines where it's appropriate installing  stronger poles shifting to a smart grid  so we can detect outages faster and more  precise locations engaging in a  vegetation management program the right  tree right place to ensure you know a  lot of the power lines are brought down  by limbs falling and so making sure that  we're minimizing those risks but even if  we achieve that all of these resiliency  gains with these approaches there will  still be outages hurricanes blizzards  ice storms they're going to take down  wires and poles and the lights are going  to go out the power may be up ninety  nine point seven percent of the time but  when it goes out it's a tremendous and  convenience to the public and a threat  to public safety and a detriment to  commerce into our economy and so we have  puzzle  around to find out what what kind of  insurance policy can we take out what  kind of system can we put in place to  moderate the burdens when those outages  occur and we found one in in the form of  microgrids microgrids are a series of  critical they're basically tiny tiny  little electrical grids they're a series  of critical facilities could be a gas  station a hospital school police station  that are linked together by electric  light distribution lines empowered by a  distributed generation system so that  they can continue to provide power when  the main grid goes down so there's these  complicated trips and transfers is what  they're called that essentially flick a  switch so that when the when the grid  has an outage that that's that little  micro grid will island itself or isolate  itself so it can continue providing  power last year we had the legislature  gave the green light to a bond funding  to support a very innovative micro grid  grant program this is the first in the  in the nation to have we are the first  to have a statewide micro grid program  which is very exciting and also a little  lonely because we've been casting about  to try to find you know answers to some  of the fundamental questions about how  to develop these there's some complex  technical and engineering challenges  that need to be overcome there's legal  and regulatory obstacles that have to be  addressed as well as how to fund them  and there are a lot of exciting examples  that out there on the military actually  has developed a lot of these but what  we've tried to do is adapt these micro  grid system so that they can support not  just public buildings but also to that  you can network together private  buildings as well critical facilities  includes your police and fire and  prisons sewage treatment plans but it  also can mean putting together a senior  center linking it with a gas station a  grocery store so that they can stay up  and running or could be a place to even  just charge your cell phone while the  grid is down  so it's been very exciting to see this  program unfold we went out with a  request for proposals we got dozens of  towns and businesses that responded this  summer we announced that we've selected  nine facilities to move forward and then  we're also that later this year in this  fall going to be announcing another  round so that we can get even more of  these micro grids developed in  Connecticut and just 11 pointed to think  about on this you know as I said cheaper  cleaner more reliable the micro grid  solution is one that improves the  reliability of our electric system but  we also wanted to achieve those other  policy goals and I think this is where  it's so important to have to be thinking  in an integrated way about how to  address the short-term impacts of  climate change and also at the same time  the long-term impacts and the long-term  planning needed to reduce a carbon  output that's contributing to climate  change so one of the criteria that we've  included when we've been developing  these micro grids is that these should  be micro grids that are not just turning  on when the the power is down but that  they are able to provide a distributed  generation solution on a 24-7 basis that  they're powered by the cleanest  generation sources possible so that not  only are they going to provide those  benefits in the event of fortunately of  an electric outage but they can also  help to offset and displace fossil fuel  generation that's running on a  continuing basis so it's a really  exciting program I just wanted to  mention that just as one example of one  of one of the initiatives that we've  been engaged in at the Department of  Energy and Environmental Protection  reflecting on our combined mission of  energy environment that's helping to  we're address some of the impacts of  climate change on our on our electric  system so thank you so much i look  forward to the questions at the end  you
 

